# Cisco Networking Lab: Router Configuration and DHCP

**Device:** Cisco Router R1  
**Technologies:** Cisco IOS, IPv4, DHCP, Subnetting  
**Dates:** October 10, 2025 – October 12, 2026

## Overview

This project documents my hands-on Cisco networking lab, covering basic router configuration and Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP). I configured router interfaces with IPv4 addresses and created DHCP pools to automate IP address assignment for two branch networks.

## 1. Basic Router Configuration

**Date:** October 10, 2025

### Objectives
- Configure the router hostname.
- Assign IPv4 addresses to interfaces.
- Enable interfaces.
- Save the running configuration.

### Configuration

```cisco
Router> enable
Router# configure terminal
Router(config)# hostname R1

R1(config)# interface FastEthernet0/0
R1(config-if)# ip address 10.0.0.1 255.255.255.252
R1(config-if)# no shutdown
R1(config-if)# exit

R1(config)# interface FastEthernet0/1
R1(config-if)# ip address 10.0.0.5 255.255.255.252
R1(config-if)# no shutdown
R1(config-if)# end

R1# copy running-config startup-config
```

### IP Addressing Table

| Interface | IP Address | Subnet Mask | CIDR |
|---|---|---|---|
| FastEthernet0/0 | 10.0.0.1 | 255.255.255.252 | /30 |
| FastEthernet0/1 | 10.0.0.5 | 255.255.255.252 | /30 |

Both interfaces use `/30` subnets, commonly used for point-to-point links.

## 2. DHCP Configuration

**Date:** October 12, 2026

### Objectives
- Configure DHCP pools for two branch networks.
- Exclude reserved gateway addresses from DHCP assignment.
- Define network addresses and default gateways.
- Automate IP address assignment for clients.

### Configuration

```cisco
R1# configure terminal

! Exclude reserved gateway addresses
R1(config)# ip dhcp excluded-address 192.168.10.1
R1(config)# ip dhcp excluded-address 192.168.64.1

! Configure DHCP for Branch 1
R1(config)# ip dhcp pool BRANCH-1
R1(dhcp-config)# network 192.168.10.0 255.255.255.128
R1(dhcp-config)# default-router 192.168.10.1
R1(dhcp-config)# exit

! Configure DHCP for Branch 2
R1(config)# ip dhcp pool BRANCH-2
R1(dhcp-config)# network 192.168.64.0 255.255.255.128
R1(dhcp-config)# default-router 192.168.64.1
R1(dhcp-config)# exit

R1(config)# end
R1# copy running-config startup-config
```

### DHCP Addressing Table

| Branch | Network Address | Subnet Mask | Default Gateway |
|---|---|---|---|
| Branch 1 | 192.168.10.0/25 | 255.255.255.128 | 192.168.10.1 |
| Branch 2 | 192.168.64.0/25 | 255.255.255.128 | 192.168.64.1 |

Each `/25` subnet contains 128 total IP addresses, including 126 usable host addresses before exclusions and other reservations.

## 3. Verification

The following Cisco IOS commands can be used to verify interface status and DHCP configuration.

```cisco
show ip interface brief
show running-config
show ip dhcp pool
show ip dhcp binding
show ip dhcp conflict
```

### Verification Checklist

- [ ] Confirm router interfaces have the correct IP addresses.
- [ ] Verify interface status is `up/up` when links are connected and operational.
- [ ] Confirm both DHCP pools are configured.
- [ ] Connect a client to each branch network.
- [ ] Verify clients receive an IP address from the correct subnet.
- [ ] Confirm clients receive the correct default gateway.
- [ ] Save the final configuration.

*Note: These are verification steps, not claims that every test has already passed.*

